@@ HOOK
:: verbatim
A man builds a test. He writes down what he expects, in public, before he looks.
Then he runs it and he loses.

Weeks later, fixing something unrelated, he finds a page his script never
downloaded. That page belongs to the side that beat him.

Fixing it turns his failure into a success. Every step of fixing it is correct.
Watch where this goes.
:: then
Say the title. Say what the channel is.
"Nothing in this video needs you to know who Girard is."
:: must
What was the Diodorus decision, and what exactly was decided?
Why can a chain of locally defensible steps end somewhere indefensible?
How does the sample relate to the pre-registration, and why is that new?
:: ref
research  S2E07 p.1   the story, July, the two bugs before it
research  S2E07 p.2   the five statements, and 13.95 -> 13.67
research  S2E07 p.3   the rule, and post-hoc exclusion criteria
research  S2E07 p.4   the length rule that was in a doc and not in the code
source    ~/Myths/CORRECTION-1.md   the five statements, in his words
context   S2E03 p.2   what a timestamp proves, for the "invisible from inside" line
deeper    Assumed Knowledge  Q13 one-tailed, Q35 ASV/KJV, not needed here

@@ PAGE 1 — THE TEXT
:: pos  Falsifiable Girard  ·  the opener
:: head A GUY FIXES A BUG. THAT IS THE WHOLE STORY.
:: ladder
1. SAY | I want to tell you about somebody fixing a small mistake. No theory, no Bible, no philosophy. A guy fixed a bug in a computer script.
2. SAY | He is testing an idea about myths. To test it he needs a pile of old Greek texts on his computer.
3. SHOW | the two piles, and the numbers on them
4. SAY | He predicted the second pile would score higher than the first. He wrote that down before he looked.
5. SAY | He was wrong. 13.95 against 13.88. He lost by four hundredths and he published that he lost.
6. ASK | Now hold that. He has already lost, in public. Written down.
7. SAY | Weeks later he is fixing something completely different, and he notices a page of one text never got downloaded. The script missed a link.
8. LAND | And the missing page belongs to the pile that beat him.
:: cold
MYTHOGRAPHY | 13.95 | he predicted LOWER
TRAGEDY | 13.88 | he predicted HIGHER

lost by | 0.04
already published | as a failure

---

what was missing | one page of Diodorus
why | a script did not follow a link
is it real | yes, part of the text
did anyone hide it | no
:: hot
"No theory yet. Just a guy and a bug."

"Say the number out loud. Nought point nought four. That is nothing. And nothing still loses."

Gear: So. Weeks later.
:: zone draw: two bars, 13.95 and 13.88, then circle the tiny gap
:: foot corpus2 · mythography n=8 · tragedy n=12 · CORRECTION-1.md
:: loop I am going to put five sentences up, and every one of them is true.

@@ PAGE 2 — THE DIFFICULTY
:: pos  the trap, at full strength
:: head FIVE SENTENCES. REJECT ONE.
:: ladder
1. SAY | I am going to read five sentences. Every one is true. Every one is what a careful person would say.
2. SHOW | the five, one at a time, do not rush
3. ASK | Which one do you reject?
4. WAIT | actually stop. let them try it.
5. SAY | Let us go along the row and try rejecting each one out loud.
6. SHOW | the four rejections, and how stupid each sounds
7. THEY | "Obviously you fix the bug. That is just being careful."
8. SAY | Yes. That is exactly what makes this hard.
9. LAND | Four of the five cannot be rejected by anybody.
:: cold
1 | The page really is part of that text.
2 | The fetcher really did have a bug, and the bug is now fixed.
3 | Fixing bugs in your pipeline is what you are supposed to do.
4 | The corrected corpus is more accurate than the one you scored.
5 | Therefore rescore with the corrected corpus.

---

reject 1 | a page of Diodorus is not part of Diodorus
reject 2 | a bug you found and fixed was not a bug
reject 3 | pipelines should be left broken
reject 4 | the corpus missing a page is the more accurate one
:: hot
"Say it in your head before I do."

"You already know which one has to go. You just cannot say why yet."

"Every one of these is what a careful person does."
:: zone draw: the row of five, cross out four
:: foot the five, from CORRECTION-1.md
:: loop So what happens if you just follow all five like a normal careful person.

@@ PAGE 3 — THE TURN
:: pos  the turn
:: head THE FAILED PREDICTION PASSES
:: ladder
1. SAY | So he does it. He follows all five. He fixes the bug and rescores.
2. SHOW | the before and after
3. SAY | 13.95 becomes 13.67. The other number does not move.
4. ASK | What just happened to his prediction?
5. WAIT | let them see it themselves
6. SAY | It passed. The thing he published as a failure is now a success, and he did nothing wrong at any point.
7. SHOW | the quote, read it slowly
8. LAND | "None of it felt like cheating."
:: cold
MYTHOGRAPHY | 13.95 | → | 13.67
TRAGEDY | 13.88 | → | 13.88

prediction | FAIL | → | PASS

---

from the correction file, written by the person it happened to:

"It would have been entirely defensible as a bug fix,
and it is exactly the move pre-registration exists
to make impossible."
:: hot
"Read it again. Not: I was tempted and I resisted."

"Not: I noticed what I was about to do."

"It did not feel like anything. That is the whole problem."
:: refrain NONE OF IT FELT LIKE CHEATING | 1 of 3
:: zone draw: arrow 13.95 down to 13.67, then across the tragedy line
:: foot 13.95 → 13.67 · tragedy 13.88 · CORRECTION-1.md
:: loop Now, here is why being a good person does not save you from this.

@@ PAGE 4 — PRESSURE, TEST ONE
:: pos  test one
:: head WHY BEING CAREFUL IS WHAT GETS YOU
:: ladder
1. ASK | If you wanted to stop this happening to you, what would you rely on?
2. THEY | "I would just be honest about it."
3. NO | Nothing here was dishonest. Not one false sentence was written.
4. THEY | "I would be careful."
5. NO | The whole sequence IS being careful. Fixing the bug was the careful act.
6. SHOW | the rest of the defences, and why each one is useless
7. ASK | So what was he actually doing at the moment it happened?
8. SAY | Maintenance. He was not looking at the prediction at all.
9. LAND | The habits that protect you everywhere else are what walk you in here.
:: cold
the defence | why it fails here
honesty | no false statement occurs anywhere
integrity | nothing presented itself as a choice about it
care | the whole sequence IS care
expertise | the chain is only visible if you watch for it

---

what he was doing | fixing a bug he had just found
what he was not doing | looking at the prediction
:: hot
"Watch which defence you reach for first. I reached for honesty."

"Every one of them is a virtue and every one of them is useless."

"If you are thinking you would have caught it, hold that. I will come back to it."
:: refrain NONE OF IT FELT LIKE CHEATING | 2 of 3
:: zone draw: the chain, link by link, no branch points
:: foot S2E3 "the shaping is invisible from the inside" · CORRECTION-1.md
:: loop There is a character everybody knows who is this exact chain.

@@ PAGE 5 — PRESSURE, TEST TWO
:: pos  test two
:: head POINT AT THE DECISION. GO ON.
:: ladder
1. SAY | Think about Walter White. Just the early episodes, before anything big.
2. ASK | Point at the scene where he decides to become a criminal.
3. WAIT | genuinely wait. they will try.
4. THEY | "The pilot? When he cooks the first batch?"
5. NO | He is dying and broke and wants to leave money behind. That is a reasonable thing for a man in that spot.
6. SAY | Every single step is a sensible answer to the step before it. There is no scene. There is a chain.
7. ASK | So how does anybody in that story know where they are?
8. SAY | In the show, you know, because you are watching from outside and you can see it going somewhere.
9. LAND | In a research project nobody is watching from outside, because the destination is a number that came out slightly different.
:: cold
no scene | in which he chooses to become a criminal
every step | a reasonable response to the step before
answer | there isn't one

---

the show | the audience can see where it goes
a research project | nobody can
the five statements | the same chain, one afternoon
:: hot
"Point at it. Go on."

"In the show you get sixty hours and a soundtrack. Here you get one afternoon and a spreadsheet."

Gear: Now watch what he actually did.
:: zone draw: chain again, label where the afternoon sits
:: foot five statements · S2E7
:: loop So what do you do, when four of five cannot be rejected.

@@ PAGE 6 — PRESSURE, TEST THREE
:: pos  test three
:: head HE KEEPS THE BROKEN VERSION ON PURPOSE
:: ladder
1. SAY | Here is what he actually did, and it is strange. He fetched the missing page. It is on his disk right now.
2. SAY | And he refuses to use it.
3. ASK | Why would anybody keep the worse version?
4. THEY | "That is stupid. You have the better data right there."
5. SAY | He rejects statement 5. Only statement 5. Only for this round.
6. SAY | So he publishes a number computed on a corpus he knows is missing a page, while the fixed corpus sits on the same machine.
7. SAY | And the page becomes available again the moment a new prediction is written down first.
8. LAND | The instrument is kept less accurate than it could be, on purpose, and that is the right call.
:: cold
the page was fetched | it is on the disk
the page is excluded | from the analysis

permanently | from this round
available to | a future round whose predictions are committed first

---

reject statement | 5
only statement | 5
only for | this round
:: hot
"Say how strange that is out loud. Do not smooth it."

"It is the right call and nothing about it feels right while you are doing it."

"You would have rescored. So would I. That is the point of the whole video."
:: refrain NONE OF IT FELT LIKE CHEATING | 3 of 3
:: zone draw: two disks, one used, one not
:: foot on disk, excluded · round 2 only
:: loop And out of this, a rule got written that had not existed before.

@@ PAGE 7 — THE LANDING
:: pos  the landing
:: head THE SAMPLE IS PART OF THE CLAIM
:: ladder
1. SAY | Everybody already knows one version of this rule.
2. SAY | You cannot change your hypothesis after you have seen the data. Everyone nods at that.
3. ASK | So why does almost nobody say the other half?
4. LAND | You cannot change your sample either.
5. SAY | Not the instrument. The sample. The texts themselves are part of what you promised.
6. SAY | And I will tell you why this one stayed with me all week.
:: cold
everyone knows | you cannot change your hypothesis after seeing the data

far fewer know | you cannot change your sample either

not just the instrument | the sample is part of the registration
:: hot
"This is the one that has bothered me since I read it."

"He is not a fraud. He is the most careful person in this story."
:: zone
:: foot CORRECTION-1.md · the rule, July 2026
:: loop
